# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reference figure for genre `flowchart` — EXPERIMENTAL GENRE (spec/genres/flowchart.md).
# Read that status first: `flowchart` is OUTSIDE the v0.1 conformance surface
# and outside the compatibility promise (`CONSTRUCT-STATUS-TIERS`), so unlike block.fd there is no
# normative/experimental split to make here — EVERY line below is outside the
# promise *as written in a flowchart document*, including the ones whose own
# status is NORMATIVE. What the split would separate is instead marked per line:
# each construct's OWN status is noted where it first appears, because that is
# what an author needs when carrying the same construct into `block`.
# The set demonstrates every FORM of every keyword, not just every keyword.
# Coverage: node tools/reference-coverage.js --strict flowchart
figdown 0.2 flowchart
title "Job dispatch"

# `class` declares a meaning ONCE; elements join it with class=. The meaning
# string is REQUIRED and it is what a reading agent gets instead of a hex value.
class retry  "Retried — the step is re-entered after a backoff" stroke=#b45309 style=dashed
class costly "Long-running — minutes, not seconds"              fill=#fef3c7
# A class may declare that it claims NO meaning (`CLASS-EMPTY-MEANING`): the empty
# string is a WRITTEN value, not an absent one. Such a class groups attributes
# and draws NO legend entry — use it when the colour really is only colour.
class tint   ""                                                 fill=#f8fafc

# `external` is the outside world an edge enters from or leaves to. It is never
# drawn as a shape (`EXTERNAL-EDGE-ENDPOINTS`). One at a SOURCE and one at a SINK, so both open ends
# of the procedure are stated rather than left to the reader to assume.
external queue "from the job queue"
external audit "to the audit log"

# ---- the role vocabulary (`FLOWCHART-ROLE-KEYWORDS`) -------------------------------
# Why words and not shapes: a reader who has never opened the spec should get
# most of a .fd from the syntax alone. `node q "Valid?" shape=diamond` needs
# the drawing convention first; `decision q "Valid?"` is simply read. And the
# convention does not even hold — of 216 question-labelled nodes measured in
# the production corpus, 78% are diamond, 14% ellipse and 8% carry no shape,
# so a reader applying it misreads about a fifth of them.
# `terminator`, `process` and `decision` are `node` with a ROLE recorded. They
# share the node id namespace and are addressable by edge/pin/rank exactly
# like a node. The geometry is DERIVED from the role — rounded, box, diamond —
# so a correct flowchart writes no shape= at all.
terminator accept "Job accepted"
process    parse  "Parse job descriptor"
# A binary branch: the condition rides the EDGE's [mid] label (see below), and
# the node's own label asks the question. Nothing else states the branch.
decision   valid  "Descriptor valid?"
terminator reject "Rejected"
# `class=` takes a comma-separated LIST: channels apply left to right, later
# wins per channel. These two touch different channels, so the fill comes from
# `costly` and the dash from `retry`.
process    fetch  "Fetch inputs" class=costly,retry

# An n-way dispatch: ONE decision with more than two labelled exits. ISO 5807
# §9.2.2.4 defines the symbol as "a single entry but where there may be a
# number of alternative exits", so three or more is the same construct, not a
# different one — and the exit VALUES ride the edges exactly as yes/no does.
decision kind "Job kind?"

# WITHDRAWN from this genre — the three processes below were wrapped in
# `group work "Per-kind work" fill=transparent style=dashed gap=0`, with
# `in=work` on each member. `group` was WITHDRAWN from `flowchart`
# (`SCENE-KEYWORD-MEMBERSHIP`) on ground (a), no evidence of need: across the whole tree the only
# `group` ever written in a flowchart document was this one, in this reference
# figure — a keyword demonstrated nowhere but in its own demonstration. It is
# KEPT in `block` (89 uses across 5 authored figures) and in `topology`, which
# is what a per-genre vocabulary is for (`SUBJECT-VOCABULARY-SCOPE`): one genre's evidence is not
# another's. What the box was saying here, `rank build,test,deploy` below still
# says, and says as CONTENT rather than as a frame.
process build  "Compile artifacts" class=costly
process test   "Run test suite"    class=costly
process deploy "Roll out release"
# A role line MAY still write `shape=` — it overrides the DRAWING only, for an
# author who needs a particular picture. The role stays the model (§12.7:
# presentation is not meaning on its own), so this is a `process` that happens
# to be drawn as an ellipse, and an unfamiliar reader still recovers "process"
# from the word rather than from the outline.
process other  "Record unknown kind" shape=ellipse class=tint

# A bare `node` under `flowchart` is ROLE-UNSTATED, and that is a FOURTH state,
# not a defaulted `process` (`UNSAFE-DEFAULT-ELIMINATION` §3 — a default that can assert a falsehood is
# not allowed). It says ONE thing: THE SOURCE DOES NOT STATE THE ROLE. Here the
# procedure says only "wait and retry", and no clause of it classifies the
# stage: ISO 5807 §9.2.1 defines Process as a function "resulting in a change
# in value, form or location of information", which a backoff is not, and which
# ISO symbol a wait belongs to is not settled in this repository (the standard
# was readable only to p. 8 of 25). So this line claims no role rather than
# claim a wrong one — and a reading agent sees the difference between "the
# author said nothing" and "the author said process".
#
# What a bare `node` is NOT: a place to park a symbol this genre cannot spell.
# Nine ISO stage symbols have no word here (Data, Stored data, Predefined
# process, Preparation, Manual operation, Manual input, Document, Parallel
# mode, Loop limit), and that is a COVERAGE GAP in FigDown, not a fact about
# the figure. When the source DOES state one of them, the interim is `node`
# PLUS a comment naming the ISO symbol, so the classification survives as text
# a reader can quote:
#
#     node cfg "Read config file"   # ISO 5807 Data (input/output) — no role
#
# and the gap gets reported — the project’s working record keeps it.
node backoff "Wait (exponential backoff)" shape=circle class=retry

# WITHDRAWN — `plane over "Overlay" z-index=1` stood here, and the terminator
# below carried `plane=over`. The line's own comment used to call `plane` "scene
# vocabulary, legal here and identical under block/topology" — that sentence is
# the model `SUBJECT-VOCABULARY-SCOPE` dissolved, and the keyword did not survive the audit it invited:
# `plane`, `z-index=` and `plane=` were WITHDRAWN from the LANGUAGE
# (`PAINT-ORDER-CONSTRUCT`), in every genre, with no replacement spelling. In this genre the ground
# was (a), no evidence of need — the only `plane` in any flowchart document in
# the tree was the one written above to cover the surface.
decision   ok   "All steps green?"
terminator done "Released"

flow down                # right|down|left|up — `down` is flowchart's default
# `rank` states that these elements are PEERS — same position along the flow
# axis. It is CONTENT ("these are alternatives"), not layout, so it survives
# the `GUI-WRITEBACK-STRUCTURE` strip test the `layout` zone below does not.
rank build,test,deploy

flowline queue -> accept
flowline accept -> parse
flowline parse -> valid
# The branch condition rides the edge's [mid] label and NOTHING else — not the
# shape, not the colour, not the rendered position (semantic model, `MEANING-RECOVERY-SOURCE`).
flowline valid -[no]-> reject
flowline valid -[yes]-> fetch
flowline fetch -> kind
# Three value-labelled exits plus a fallback: the dispatch value is written on
# each edge, so a reading agent can answer "what happens for a test job?".
flowline kind -[build]-> build
flowline kind -[test]-> test
flowline kind -[deploy]-> deploy
flowline kind -[other]-> other
flowline build -> ok
flowline test -> ok
flowline deploy -> ok
flowline other -> ok
flowline ok -[yes]-> done
# The CYCLE. FigDown has no loop keyword and needs none: a plain back-edge to
# an earlier step IS the loop, and its [mid] label is the loop condition. A
# `loop`/`while` construct would state nothing these two lines do not.
flowline ok -[no]-> backoff class=retry
flowline backoff -> fetch class=retry
flowline done -> audit
flowline reject -> audit

# Everything below is geometry with no meaning, and a reading agent skips it by
# default (`CONTENT-LAYOUT-ZONE-SPLIT`/`GENRE-NAMESPACE`). Removing the whole zone leaves the same figure meaning.
layout
pin queue at=(150,0)              # an external is pinnable exactly like a node
pin other width=130              # width only
pin backoff width=120 height=40  # both
